.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Born and Lande (1918) balanced the Madelung attraction -A/r against a short-range repulsion B/r^n; setting dU/dr=0 at the observed spacing r_0 gives U=-\frac{N_AM|z_+z_-|e^2}{4\pi\varepsilon_0r_0}(1-\frac{1}{n}), which BornLandeLatticeEnergy evaluates. This example shows the energy curve for NaCl and compares the equation with Born-Haber values for six rock-salt halides.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/crystal/lattice_energy/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_born_lande_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/crystal/lattice_energy/plot_01_born_lande`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Born-Lande equation: lattice energies of the alkali halides</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/crystal/lattice_energy/plot_01_born_lande`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Madelung (1918) asked for the electrostatic energy of one ion in the field of every other ion of an infinite crystal. For rock salt it is U=-Me^2/(4\pi\varepsilon_0r_0) per ion pair, with the Madelung constant M=\sum_j \pm r_0/r_j summed shell by shell: 6 opposite charges at r_0, 12 like charges at \sqrt2r_0, 8 opposite charges at \sqrt3r_0, and so on. madelung_constant_nacl returns the converged value.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/crystal/madelung/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_madelung_lattice_sum_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/crystal/madelung/plot_02_madelung_lattice_sum`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Madelung's lattice sum: the electrostatic energy of rock salt</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/crystal/madelung/plot_02_madelung_lattice_sum`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Roothaan and Hall turned the molecular orbital problem into matrix algebra: expand each orbital in a finite, non-orthogonal basis and the variational condition becomes the generalized eigenvalue problem HC=SCE, solved here by solve_secular_equation. For the one-electron H2+ the Fock matrix is just the core Hamiltonian, so the equation is solved exactly once in each basis. The example shows that the overlap matrix S matters, that Lowdin&#x27;s symmetric orthogonalization S^{-1/2}HS^{-1/2} gives the same answer, and that enlarging the basis lowers the energy toward the exact H2+ value, as the variational principle requires.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hartree_fock/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_05_roothaan_hall_secular_equation_thumb.png
